*** Spoilers abound in this message, so if you don't want to be
spoiled, read no further. ***

I've been playing for about a year and a half now, and finally just
ascended my first character in 3.4.3.  It was a male elven wizard. 
Originally I'd been playing neutral human barbarians, but after a few
annoying deaths involving my lack of sleep resistance, I went elf. 
Spellcasters take a little more practice, but elven wizards tend to
become very powerful towards mid-game.

The game started off without a bang . . . no magic marker, not a
particularly useful 2nd spell (confuse monster), poor scrolls and
rings too (+1 ring of increase damage was the best thing).  After not
too long, though, I found a ring of slow digestion, which helps my
game quite a lot (not working against the clock is very nice for
building a stong character).

Almost before I went anywhere, I prayed too early and made Anhur
angry.  This situation lasted quite some time (it's especially tough
for low-level chaotics to mollify a diety).

I got a pretty early throne wish, which went to blessed +2 silver
dragon scale mail.  I went for silver because I wanted to leave my
amulet slot open for life saving (I fall into the "poor typist"
category when it comes to YASD's).  The next wish was a magic lamp
from the Mines, and it brought me that amulet of life saving.  Things
were looking pretty good, except that Anhur was still angry with me.

I cleaned out the mines, got the luckstone from Mines' End, and
returned to Mine Town.  I finally was able to mollify Anhur by
sacrificing a shopkeeper.  I cleaned out Mine Town and bought lots of
protection.

Once Anhur was happy, I set up my main camp near the altar at Level 1
(since I knew I'd be getting the Eye of Aethiopica sooner or later,
Level 1 is actually a pretty easy location to work from).  I spent a
great deal of time sacrificing and praying, and built up a pretty good
collection of spellbooks, as well as getting Magicbane, crowned, all
the prayer intrinsics, lots more protection, and the castle tune.  The
sacrifice/pray cycle was helped a lot when a werewolf showed up . . .
I simply placed him next to the altar, and stayed right next to him
while he created many wolves and winter wolves.  He finally died at
the hands of one of his own winter wolves, but not before I'd
sacrificed over 300 of his buddies.  I also managed to sacrifice a
couple of elves, and escorted both Juiblex and Yeenoghu down to the
Mines where they wouldn't bother anybody.

I found a good bones pile near level 10 or so in the main dungeon,
which provided a bag of holding.  I never did do Sokoban, because if
you've already got reflection and a bag of holding, well, it's just
annoying.

Another wish from a throne brought blessed +2 speed boots.  Combat
suddenly got a lot easier.  I went on to do the Quest without any
major issues.

By now I'd found rings of polymorph and polymorph control, and decided
to try stocking up cockatrice eggs for later.  Here I made my 1st
mistake: I left my amulet of life saving, along with all my other main
inventory, on an Elbereth square while I did the polyselfing.  At some
point, a soldier came along, didn't respect Elbereth, and stole that
amulet and Magicbane.  I was naked, and he was attacking me, so I had
to kill him (twice).  In restrospect I should have tried polymorphing
into a nymph to steal the amulet of life saving back.  Other than
that, though, I made 27 cockatrice eggs and improved myself
considerably (after becoming "a new elf!" a bunch of times, I noticed
I'd somehow gone from Level 17 to Level 28, with HP and energy to
match.  And this was before even reaching Medusa!)

On the way to Medusa, I found Fort Ludios for the first time.  Cleaned
it out, gained my last missing intrinsic (disintegration), stashed all
the loot back at Level 1, and headed back down.  I took care of Medusa
. . . after the first zap, she headed downstairs to a maze level, and
I quickly dispatched her with magic missiles.  I went ahead to the
Castle and played the tune . . . the plan was to keep opening and
closing the drawbridge to kill monsters as they tried to come out, but
an inconsiderate giant had left a boulder blocking the doorway.  So I
just waltzed in and killed them all in melee instead.  The soldiers
were no problem, likewise the dragons.  The best levitation apparatus
I had was some -3 boots, but they did the job.  As soon as I laid
hands on that wand of wishing, I replaced my amulet of life saving and
breathed a sigh of relief.  Then I made my 2nd mistake . . . I ate a
dragon corpse which made me satiated, then immediately ate a single
fortune cookie.  YASD.  Good thing I was wearing the new amulet of
life saving, which I promptly replaced again.

I stepped into the Valley of the Dead so that Gehennom would show up
on the Eye's menu, then invoked the Eye to head back up to camp.  Now
it was time to polypile, enchant armor, and get everything ready for
the final descent.  Polypiling didn't turn out all that great . . . I
started with a dozen unicorn horns and a large number of spellbooks,
but I never did get any magic markers or two of the spells I really
wanted (identify and magic mapping).  I was able to make up for those
deficiencies, though, by wishing for two magic markers.  I also
decided to wish for Frost Bane.  It took two wishes.  I also took this
opportunity to genocide L, h, and ;.  Finally, I got a badly needed
ring of levitation (I wasn't looking forward to taking off my speed
boots on the Plane of Air).

I made two armor mistakes in a row . . . I didn't fully understand how
scrolls of enchant armor work, and blew up a +5 T-shirt, followed by a
+4 pair of gauntlets of dexterity.  Now I know . . . don't enchant
anymore after it's above +3.  You live and learn.  Nonetheless, I had
AC -38 by the time I left for Gehennom (naked AC 0).

I was so overprepared that Gehennom was uneventful.  I used scrolls of
magic mapping on nearly every level, used my first cockatrice egg on
Vlad (his throne let me genocide green slimes) and my 2nd on Orcus,
and hit the bottom at level 48.  Finding the vibrating square took a
while, but I finally found it, marked it, headed for the Wizard,
stoned him too, and returned to do the invocation.  It took a few
minutes to slog through all the monsters in Moloch's Sanctum, but
another egg quickly dispatched the High Priest, giving me the Amulet. 
*THE* Amulet.  I'd been saving a cursed potion of gain level for ages,
and used it to get the hell out of there before the many monsters I'd
avoided started coming through the door.  I was really starting to
believe this would be a successful ascension, since I was wearing one
amulet of life saving, and by now had two more stashed away along with
a stack of cockatrice eggs and the Finger of Death.

Rodney came back to see me a total of 5 more times, and I gave him the
finger each time.  He went down every time without fail.  The
"mysterious force" was really annoying, and I probably crossed 15
extra levels because of it.  After leaving Gehennom, though, the rest
of the trip up was smooth as silk.

Stopping at my Level 1 camp one last time, I made sure I had
everything ready . . . 4 scolls of detect gold, check.  Lots of
K-rations, check.  Blessed potions of full healing, check.  Amulets of
life saving, check.  Spells all recently recharged, check.  I was
ready to do this.

Up I went to the Plane of Earth.  It took a total of about 30 turns,
thanks to a confused scroll of detect gold and a wand of digging.

The Plane of Air was the toughest part of my incredibly easy ascension
run . . . I think the air elementals brought me below 400 HP for the
one and only time.  After that I made pets of 6 of them.

The Plane of Fire was easy and mostly uneventful.  An Archon showed
up, which was annoying, but I put on a towel and took him down with
Frost Bane before too many of his friends showed up.

No troubles on the Plane of Water since I'd genocided all ;, but it
took a long time to find the portal since it was hidden under a stupid
potion of hallucination.

Stepped into the Astral Plane.  This was it, the moment I'd been
waiting for.  I put on my towel and checked out the landscape . . .
Famine to my left, Pestilence straight ahead, Death to my right.  I
figured I'd try to avoid Death for as long as possible, and headed
left.  This turned out to be a superb choice, as the left altar was
Anhur's.  I gave Famine the finger one time, and that was it.

Once I reached the altar, I stood there for a few moments to fully
appreciate what I was about to do.  Finally, I sacrificed the Amulet,
the choir sang, and the rest is history.




